        card("Culture note: extreme personalism", "Filipinos view the world in terms of personal relationships, and the extent to which one is able personally to relate to things and people determines our recognition of their existence and their value. There is no separation between an objective task and emotional involvement. This personalism is manifested in the tendency to give personal interpretations to actions, that is, to take things personally. Thus a sincere question may be viewed as a challenge to one’s competence, or positive feedback may be interpreted as a sign of special affection. There is, in fact, some basis for such interpretations, as Filipinos become personal in their criticism and praise. Personalism is also manifested in the need to establish personal relationships before any business or work relationship can be successful. Personal contacts are involved in any transaction and are difficult to turn down. Preference is usually given to family and friends in hiring, delivery of services, and even in voting. Extreme personalism thus leads to the graft and corruption evident in Philippine society."),
